underflow, and exact-boundary values; subclasses and alternate implementations; omitted versus explicit defaults; expected exceptions and messages; and interactions with existing public APIs. For every row, compare the old and new behavior explicitly, including return values, exception types and messages, ordering, mutation, numerical precision, serialization, and subclass or alternate-backend behavior. Mark each difference as required or accidental, add a focused regression test for every required difference, and add compatibility tests for nearby unchanged rows while preserving unrelated behavior. After every nontrivial edit, run the narrowest relevant test or a focused reproduction, inspect the complete failure output, and iterate on the implementation until the focused checks pass. Then run the broader relevant test groups and, before finalizing, perform an adversarial compatibility review covering public API signatures, exception types and messages, ordering, mutability, numerical precision, serialization, subclass behavior, and interactions with neighboring features; reject any change lacking evidence for intentional behavior differences.
